1,2)-(1,3) (row 1 columns 2-3) is a 1x2 rectangle. Another rectangle covering (2,1)-(2,3) except (2,2) is not a rectangle because you'd have a hole. So cannot. Maybe use a 2x2 rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3) but (2,2) is uncovered, so can't. So seems 4 is minimal. So lower bound seems tight.\n\nThus optimality appears correct. The lower bound proof seems valid. We need to check if any hidden errors exist. Let's examine the lower bound proof in detail.\n\nThe solution defines maximal rectangles: rectangles that cannot be enlarged without intersecting an uncovered square. Then they argue each maximal rectangle can be assigned to an uncovered square either above it (t(R)) or left of it (ℓ(R)). Since each maximal rectangle must have at least one of these squares defined (otherwise could enlarge), they assign each rectangle to a unique uncovered square.\n\nThen they argue each uncovered square can be assigned at most two rectangles: one via top side (i.e., rectangle below it) and one via left side (rectangle to its right). Actually they say: if assigned via top side, rectangle's rows are {p+1,...,p+h} and columns are {q,...,q+w}. The choice of h determines rectangle uniquely, and maximality forces h to be maximal possible, so at most one rectangle can be assigned to u in this way. Similarly for left side. So each uncovered square can be assigned at most two rectangles.\n\nThen they consider the uncovered squares in first row (p=1) cannot have rectangle assigned via top side (since no row above). Similarly first column cannot have rectangle assigned via left side. So those two squares can be assigned at most one rectangle each.\n\nThus total number of rectangles T >= 2(n-2) + 1 + 1 = 2n-2.\n\nWe need to check if any hidden assumption fails. For each uncovered square u, they claim at most one rectangle can be assigned via top side. Let's examine: Suppose we have uncovered square at (p,q). A rectangle assigned via top side must have its top side just below u, i.e., its rows start at p+1. The rectangle's columns must include q (since ℓ(R) is left side? Wait, they defined t(R) as uncovered square immediately above the top side of R (if exists). So if t(R) = (p,q), then the rectangle's top side is row p+1, and its columns must include q (the column of the uncovered square). They also say columns are exactly {q,...,q+w} for some w≥0. So rectangle's leftmost column is q. So rectangle is anchored at (p+1,q) as top-left corner. The rectangle can extend downwards and rightwards. The maximality condition forces h to be maximal possible (height). So there is at most one rectangle with top side anchored at (p+1,q). Indeed, if there were two distinct rectangles assigned via top side to the same uncovered square, they'd have different heights or widths, but maximality would be violated because you could enlarge the smaller one to match the larger one (since both would be disjoint from uncovered squares). So at most one rectangle assigned via top side. Similarly for left side.\n\nThus each uncovered square can be assigned at most two rectangles: one below it (via top side) and one to its right (via left side). That seems correct.\n\nNow they sum: there are n uncovered squares. For each of the n-2 squares not in first row or first column, each can be assigned at most two rectangles, so total assignments ≤ 2(n-2). The two special squares (first row and first column) can be assigned at most one each, adding 2. So total assignments ≤ 2n-2. Since each rectangle is assigned to exactly one uncovered square, the total number of rectangles T is at most the total assignments? Wait, they claim each rectangle can be assigned to an uncovered square. So each rectangle corresponds to one assignment. So total number of rectangles T ≤ total assignments? Actually they count assignments: each rectangle assigned to a square, so number of rectangles equals number of assignments. So T ≤ total assignments? No, each rectangle yields one assignment, so number of