 all non‑diagonal squares.  \nHence we have a tiling with  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{m_{\\min}\\le 2n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are necessary  \n\nFix an arbitrary permutation \\(p\\) and a tiling \\(\\mathcal R\\) of the complement of \\(U\\).\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 1 (left‑hand part).  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{\\,i\\mid p(i)>k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in S_k\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) lie **strictly to the left** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nAny rectangle that contains any of these squares is completely contained in the column set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a rectangle \\(R\\) contained a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in S_k\\) and \\(j\\le k\\) and also contained a column \\(c>k\\), then \\(R\\) would also contain the square \\((i,p(i))\\) because \\(p(i)>k\\) and the rectangle is convex in the grid. That square is uncovered, contradicting the definition of a tile. ∎\n\nConsequently, a rectangle that covers a square of column \\(k\\) on the left side cannot cover any square of column \\(k+1\\) on the left side, because its rightmost column would be \\(k\\).\n\nTherefore **different values of \\(k\\) require different rectangles** on the left side.  \nSince \\(k\\) runs through \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all left‑hand squares.\n\n---\n\n##### Lemma 2 (right‑hand part).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{\\,i\\mid p(i)<k\\,\\}\\qquad (k=2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\in T_k\\) and \\(k\\le j\\le n\\) lie **strictly to the right** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nAn argument symmetric to Lemma 1 shows that a rectangle covering any of these squares must be wholly contained in columns \\(\\{k,\\dots ,n\\}\\); hence a rectangle that covers a square of column \\(k\\) on the right side cannot also cover a square of column \\(k-1\\) on the right side.\n\nThus distinct values of \\(k\\) (now \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\)) again force distinct rectangles on the right side, giving another \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n---\n\nCombining Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 we obtain\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of Section 1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and the lower‑bound argument of Section 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the minimum number of rectangular tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
