rectangular tiles,\nno two tiles overlapping.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Decomposition into left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery tile lies completely inside \\(L\\) or completely inside \\(R\\); a tile\ncannot contain a cell of the form \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Row‑lengths are all distinct  \n\nIn row \\(i\\) the number of cells belonging to \\(L\\) equals \\(\\pi(i)-1\\).\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\pi(i)-1\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains each integer exactly once.  Hence the rows of \\(L\\) have pairwise\ndifferent lengths.  The same argument applied to \\(R\\) gives that the row‑\nlengths of \\(R\\) are \\(\\{n-\\pi(i)\\mid i\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), also all\ndistinct.\n\nThus both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are Ferrers (Young) boards whose row‑lengths are a\npermutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for a Ferrers board with distinct row‑lengths  \n\nConsider any Ferrers board \\(F\\) whose rows (from top to bottom) have\ndistinct lengths \\(a_1<a_2<\\dots <a_m\\) (some may be zero).  \nTake the topmost non‑empty row; its length is \\(a_k>0\\).  \nThe maximal rectangle that can be placed with its upper side on that row and\nits right side extending to column \\(a_k\\) is forced: it must occupy exactly\nthe cells  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le a_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nRemoving this rectangle leaves a smaller Ferrers board whose set of row\nlengths is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,a_k-1\\}\\setminus\\{a_k\\}\\); in particular one\npositive length disappears.  Repeating the argument shows that each distinct\npositive row length forces a separate rectangle.  C