d to the reduced board gives at least \\((n-2)\\) further rectangles.  Hence at least \\((n-1)\\) rectangles altogether. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The same bound for the right region  \n\nDefine \\(r_i = n-\\pi(i)\\) (the number of cells to the right of the hole in row \\(i\\)).  \nAgain \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nExactly the same reasoning as in Lemma 3, applied to the right region \\(R\\), shows that any tiling of \\(R\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nSince the two regions are disjoint and each rectangle lies wholly in one of them, the total number of rectangles in a tiling of the whole board is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nQ_c^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i\\le c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(Q_c^{\\text{L}}\\) lies entirely to the left of the diagonal, contains no uncovered cell, and the family \\(\\{Q_c^{\\text{L}}\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) covers every cell of \\(L\\) exactly once.\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nQ_c^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid c+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain each \\(Q_c^{\\text{R}}\\) lies to the right of the diagonal, avoids all uncovered cells, and the family \\(\\{Q_c^{\\text{R}}\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) covers every cell of \\(R\\) exactly once.\n\nThe two families together consist of  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles, and they tile the whole board except the diagonal holes.  \n\nThus a tiling with exactly \