ation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   *Left region*: for each column \\(c=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical rectangle covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,c-1\\) in column \\(c\\).  \n   *Right region*: for each column \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical rectangle covering rows \\(c+1,\\dots ,n\\) in column \\(c\\).  \n\n   Each of these rectangles is axis\u2011parallel, contains no diagonal hole, and the collection of all \\(2n-2\\) rectangles covers every non\u2011diagonal cell exactly once.  \n\n   Hence a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA unit square of the \\(n\\times n\\) board is identified by its row and column indices \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince every row and every column must contain **exactly one** uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two disjoint Young diagrams  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) consists of the cells that lie **to the left** of the hole in their row; \\(R\\) consists of the cells that lie **to the right** of the hole in their row.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus U = L\\cup R,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* Every axis\u2011parallel rectangle that avoids all points of \\(U\\) is contained entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.* Let a rectangle \\(Q\\) avoid \\(U\\) and intersect two distinct rows \\(i_1,i_2\\).  \nBecause the columns of \\(Q\\) form a contiguous interval, either all columns of \\(Q\\) are \\(<\\pi(i_1)\\) or all are \\(>\\pi(i_1)\\); otherwise column \\(\\pi(i_1)\\) would lie inside \\(Q\\) and the cell \\((i_1,\\pi(i_1))\\) would be covered, contradicting the hypothesis.  \nThe same reasoning with row \\(i_2\\) shows that the same side (left or right) holds for row \\(i_2\\).  \nHence all rows intersected by \\(Q\\) lie on the same side of their respective holes, i.e. \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) or \\(Q\\subseteq R\\). \u220e\n\nThus the tiling problem separates into two independent sub\u2011problems: tiling \\(L\\) and tiling \\(R\\) with rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i = |\\{j\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\}| = \\pi(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nConsequently exactly one row has length \\(n-1\\); denote this row by \\(r\\).\n\nConsider the right\u2011most cell of row \\(r\\), namely \\((r,n-1)\\).  \nNo other row contains column \\(n-1\\) (all other rows have length at most \\(n-2\\)).  \nHence any rectangle covering \\((r,n-1)\\) cannot contain any cell of another row; it must be confined to row \\(r\\) alone.  \nTherefore at least one rectangle is required for row \\(r\\).\n\nDelete row \\(r\\) and the cells of that rectangle.  \nThe remaining left region consists of \\(n-1\\) rows whose lengths are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  \nApplying the same argument recursively yields the following statement.\n\n*Lemma.*  The left region \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles in any tiling.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*  \nFor \\(n=1\\) the statement is trivial.  \nAssume it holds for size \\(n-1\\).  \nIn the size\u2011\\(n\\) case the row of length \\(n-1\\) forces a dedicated rectangle, and the induction hypothesis applied to the reduced board gives at least \\((n-2)\\) further rectangles.  Hence at least \\((n-1)\\) rectangles altogether. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The same bound for the right region  \n\nDefine \\(r_i = n-\\pi(i)\\) (the number of cells to the right of the hole in row \\(i\\)).  \nAgain \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nExactly the same reasoning as in Lemma\u202f3, applied to the right region \\(R\\), shows that any tiling of \\(R\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nSince the two regions are disjoint and each rectangle lies wholly in one of them, the total number of rectangles in a tiling of the whole board is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nQ_c^{\\text{L}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i\\le c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(Q_c^{\\text{L}}\\) lies entirely to the left of the diagonal, contains no uncovered cell, and the family \\(\\{Q_c^{\\text{L}}\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) covers every cell of \\(L\\) exactly once.\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nQ_c^{\\text{R}}=\\{(i,c)\\mid c+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAgain each \\(Q_c^{\\text{R}}\\) lies to the right of the diagonal, avoids all uncovered cells, and the family \\(\\{Q_c^{\\text{R}}\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) covers every cell of \\(R\\) exactly once.\n\nThe two families together consist of  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles, and they tile the