"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Graph\u2011theoretic reformulation** \u2013  \n   Identify rows and columns with the two colour classes of the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{n,n}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)).  \n   The unit squares correspond to edges.  \n   Leaving exactly one uncovered square in each row and column is equivalent to deleting a perfect matching \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   A rectangular tile covering a set of rows \\(R'\\) and columns \\(C'\\) corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph (a *biclique*) with vertex sets \\(R'\\) and \\(C'\\); the tile is admissible only when it contains no deleted edge, i.e. \\(C'\\subseteq [n]\\setminus\\sigma(R')\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound via matrix rank** \u2013  \n   After deleting the matching, the adjacency matrix of the remaining graph is \\(A=J-I\\) (all\u2011ones matrix minus the identity).  \n   Over \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) we have \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\).  \n   The Boolean rank of a \\(0\\!-\\!1\\) matrix (the smallest number of rank\u20111 Boolean matrices whose entry\u2011wise sum equals the matrix) is a lower bound for the number of bicliques needed to **cover** its 1\u2011entries; a partition is a special case of a cover, so the same lower bound applies to our problem.  \n   Hence at least \\(n\\) tiles are necessary.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound** \u2013  \n   Choose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   For each row \\(i\\) place a single tile that covers the whole row except the diagonal cell, i.e. the rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile.  \n   The \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint (they occupy distinct rows) and together cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, giving exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.\n\n4. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, therefore the minimum number of tiles equals \\(n=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the set of rows by \\(R=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the set of columns by \\(C=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nA unit square is identified with the ordered pair \\((r,c)\\in R\\times C\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reformulation as a biclique\u2011partition problem  \n\nMatilda must leave exactly one square uncovered in each row and each column.  \nConsequently there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma:R\\to C\\) such that the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(r,\\sigma(r))\\mid r\\in R\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered, and a rectangular tile whose set of rows is\n\\(R'\\subseteq R\\) and whose set of columns is \\(C'\\subseteq C\\) covers precisely the\nCartesian product \\(R'\\times C'\\).  \nThe tile is admissible only if it contains no uncovered square, i.e.\n\n\\[\nR'\\times C'\\cap U=\\varnothing\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\nC'\\subseteq C\\setminus\\sigma(R').\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus each admissible tile corresponds to a *complete bipartite subgraph*\n(biclique) with left vertex set \\(R'\\) and right vertex set \\(C'\\) satisfying (2).\n\nMatilda\u2019s task is therefore to partition the edge set  \n\n\\[\nE:=\\bigl(R\\times C\\bigr)\\setminus U\n\\]\n\ninto the smallest possible number of such bicliques.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound via the rank of the adjacency matrix  \n\nWrite the adjacency matrix \\(A\\) of the bipartite graph \\((R\\cup C,E)\\) with rows indexed by \\(R\\) and columns by \\(C\\).  \nBecause exactly the pairs \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\) are missing,  \n\n\\[\nA = J - I ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the \\(n\\times n\\) all\u2011ones matrix and \\(I\\) is the identity matrix.\nThe eigenvalues of \\(J-I\\) are \\(n-1\\) (multiplicity \\(1\\)) and \\(-1\\) (multiplicity \\(n-1\\));\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb{R}}(A)=n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor a \\(0\\!-\\!1\\) matrix, the *Boolean rank* (the smallest number of rank\u20111 Boolean matrices whose entry\u2011wise sum equals the given matrix) is a lower bound for the number of bicliques needed to **cover** its 1\u2011entries.  \nA partition of the 1\u2011entries is a special case of a cover, therefore the same lower bound applies to any partition.  \nSince Boolean rank \\(\\ge\\) ordinary real rank, (3) implies that any biclique partition of \\(E\\) must contain at least \\(n\\) bicliques.\n\nConsequently **at least \\(n\\) rectangular tiles are necessary**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) to be the identity, i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile; its row set is \\(\\{i\\}\\) and its column set is\n\\(C\\setminus\\{i\\}\\), which satisfies condition (2) because the only forbidden column for row \\(i\\) is precisely \\(i\\).\n\nThe tiles \\(\\{T_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint (they occupy distinct rows) and together cover every square of the grid except the diagonal squares, which are exactly the required uncovered squares.  \n\nThus **\\(n\\) tiles are sufficient**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nFrom Section\u202f2 we have a lower bound of \\(n\\) tiles, and Section\u202f3 provides a\nconstruction using exactly \\(n\\) tiles.  Hence the lower bound is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Result for the given grid  \n\nWith \\(n=2025\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}\n\\]\n\nas the minimum number of rectangular tiles required.\n\n**Explicit example (for the given size).**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,2025\\}\\) place a tile covering all unit squares in row \\(i\\) except the square in column \\(i\\).  \nThese \\(2025\\) tiles satisfy the condition that every row and every column has exactly one uncovered unit square (the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\)).  \n\nThis construction achieves the proven minimum."
